1
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:13,830
Most tokens arrive the same way.
A ticker, a supply, and somebody's word for it.

2
00:00:16,200 --> 00:00:20,780
Folio starts from the other end:
the listing is the article.

3
00:00:21,400 --> 00:00:26,400
Signed by its author, and priced at
the foot of the page.

4
00:00:27,300 --> 00:00:29,000
Publishing takes one transaction.

5
00:00:29,400 --> 00:00:34,400
One call to the factory.
No listing fee, no pool to seed.

6
00:00:34,700 --> 00:00:39,700
What comes back is an ERC-20
that is its own market maker.

7
00:00:40,400 --> 00:00:45,820
Price comes off a bonding curve,
quoted by the contract as you read.

8
00:00:46,200 --> 00:00:52,450
Holders buy from it, and sell straight back
into its reserve — no counterparty to find.

9
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:58,000
None of that is enforced by
a component that could skip it.

10
00:00:58,400 --> 00:01:02,570
It lives in row-level security,
and in the contract itself.

11
00:01:03,900 --> 00:01:06,400
One thing an intro shouldn't bury.

12
00:01:06,600 --> 00:01:09,930
This is a mainnet. The money is real.

13
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:11,800
Folio.

14
00:01:12,400 --> 00:01:14,500
Write the piece. Publish it.

15
00:01:14,800 --> 00:01:17,300
Every token, told as a story.
